00:26:55.000 --> 00:27:38.000
holding the hand of a loved one. And that's just one example of the way that relationships might be able to affect our physical health and in this case our brain activity in a pretty profound way. So I think we're beginning to understand that with the help of these new technologies. It's very exciting. I'm eager to see sort of where it takes us in the future. There must have been some influence by participating in the study, that there was a connection, say, between the researcher and the participant. Do you have any sense of how much that may have influenced the outcome? I mean, people from your book, some of the people who were interviewed actually looked forward to the regular interactions. You know, has that skewed things in a way that would make a difference, do you think?
握着爱人的手时。这只是人际关系可能能够以一种相当深刻的方式影响我们的身体健康，在这种情况下是我们的大脑活动的一个例子。所以我认为我们开始在这些新技术的帮助下理解这一点。这非常令人兴奋。我很渴望看到它在未来会把我们带向何方。参与这项研究一定有一些影响，即研究人员和参与者之间存在联系。您对这在多大程度上影响了结果有什么感觉吗？我的意思是，您书中的人，一些被采访的人实际上期待着定期的互动。你知道，这是否以一种会产生差异的方式扭曲了事情，您认为？

